  <page title="Virginia Lottery prizes" route="/your-taxes/virginia/virginia-lottery">
    <section>
      <div class="df-screen" condition="/vaIsResident" operator="isTrue">
        <h2>Did you win a Virginia Lottery prize of less than $600 in 2024?</h2>
        <p>Virginia does not tax prizes under $600, but your federal return counts them as income, so Virginia takes them back out on Line 7 (Va. Code § 58.1-322.02(7)). Prizes of $600 or more are reported to Virginia and stay taxable. No one reports the small ones, so only you can tell us.</p>
        <fg-set path="/vaHasLotteryPrizesUnder600">
          <question>Did you win a Virginia Lottery prize of less than $600 in 2024?</question>
          <input type="boolean">
            <option value="true">Yes</option>
            <option value="false">No</option>
          </input>
        </fg-set>
      </div>
      <div class="df-screen" condition="/vaHasLotteryPrizesUnder600" operator="isTrue">
        <h2>How much did those prizes add up to?</h2>
        <p>Count only prizes that were each less than $600.</p>
        <fg-set path="/vaLotteryPrizesUnder600">
          <question>Total of Virginia Lottery prizes under $600</question>
          <input type="dollar"/>
        </fg-set>
      </div>
    </section>
  </page>
  <page title="Commonwealth Savers contributions" route="/your-taxes/virginia/virginia-savers">
    <section>
      <div class="df-screen" condition="/vaIsResident" operator="isTrue">
        <h2>Did you put money into a Commonwealth Savers (Virginia529) account in 2024?</h2>
        <p>Commonwealth Savers is the state's college savings plan. Contributions are deductible on Line 13, up to $4,000 per account for the year unless you have reached age 70 (Va. Code § 58.1-322.03(7)). The plan reports nothing to the IRS, so only you can tell us.</p>
        <fg-set path="/vaHasCommonwealthSaversContributions">
          <question>Did you contribute to a Commonwealth Savers account in 2024?</question>
          <input type="boolean">
            <option value="true">Yes</option>
            <option value="false">No</option>
          </input>
        </fg-set>
      </div>
      <div class="df-screen" condition="/vaHasCommonwealthSaversContributions" operator="isTrue">
        <h2>How much did you contribute in 2024?</h2>
        <p>Enter the total. The deduction is capped at $4,000 unless you have reached 70; anything above that would carry forward to later years in a real return.</p>
        <fg-set path="/vaCommonwealthSaversContributions">
          <question>Amount contributed to Commonwealth Savers accounts</question>
          <input type="dollar"/>
        </fg-set>
      </div>
    </section>
  </page>
  <page title="Untaxed purchases" route="/your-taxes/virginia/virginia-use-tax">
    <section>
      <div class="df-screen" condition="/vaIsResident" operator="isTrue">
        <h2>Did you buy anything in 2024 without paying Virginia sales tax?</h2>
        <p>Online, by mail, or in another state, where the seller charged no Virginia tax. Virginia asks every filer this (Line 33, consumer's use tax). No seller reports it, so the answer has to be yours. Where you live sets the rate: 5.3% in most of Virginia, 6% in Northern Virginia, Hampton Roads and the Richmond region, 6.3% or 7% in a few localities.</p>
        <fg-set path="/vaHasUntaxedPurchases">
          <question>Did you buy anything without paying Virginia sales tax in 2024?</question>
          <input type="boolean">
            <option value="true">Yes</option>
            <option value="false">No, nothing untaxed</option>
          </input>
        </fg-set>
      </div>
      <div class="df-screen" condition="/vaHasUntaxedPurchases" operator="isTrue">
        <h2>How much did those purchases add up to?</h2>
        <p>Separately stated shipping is not taxable. Your use tax is this total times the rate for your locality.</p>
        <fg-set path="/vaUntaxedPurchases">
          <question>Total of purchases on which no Virginia sales tax was paid</question>
          <input type="dollar"/>
        </fg-set>
      </div>
    </section>
  </page>
  <page title="Form 760, computed from your federal return" route="/your-taxes/virginia/virginia-summary">
    <section>
      <div class="df-screen" condition="/vaIsResident" operator="isTrue">
        <h2>Form 760, computed from your federal return</h2>
        <p><strong>Demonstration only.</strong> Tax year <fg-show path="/taxYear"/>. Each line names the law it comes from. Locality code <fg-show path="/vaLocalityCode"/>.</p>
        <table class="usa-table usa-table--borderless">
          <caption>Virginia Form 760</caption>
          <tr><th scope="row">Line 1, federal adjusted gross income</th><td><fg-show path="/vaFederalAdjustedGrossIncome"/></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Line 4, age deduction (§ 58.1-322.03(5))</th><td><fg-show path="/vaAgeDeduction"/></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Line 5, taxable Social Security benefits (§ 58.1-322.02(3))</th><td><fg-show path="/vaTaxableSocialSecurityBenefits"/></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Line 7, Virginia Lottery prizes under $600 (§ 58.1-322.02(7))</th><td><fg-show path="/vaLotterySubtraction"/></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Line 9, Virginia adjusted gross income</th><td><fg-show path="/vaAdjustedGrossIncome"/></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Line 11, standard deduction (§ 58.1-322.03(1)(b))</th><td><fg-show path="/vaStandardDeduction"/></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Line 12, exemptions (§ 58.1-322.03(2))</th><td><fg-show path="/vaExemptions"/></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Line 13, Commonwealth Savers deduction (§ 58.1-322.03(7))</th><td><fg-show path="/vaCommonwealthSaversDeduction"/></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Line 15, Virginia taxable income</th><td><fg-show path="/vaTaxableIncome"/></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Line 16, Virginia income tax (§ 58.1-320)</th><td><strong><fg-show path="/vaTax"/></strong></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Line 19a, Virginia tax withheld (W-2 box 17)</th><td><fg-show path="/vaWithholding"/></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Lines 20 to 22, payments Virginia Tax has on record</th><td><fg-show path="/vaEstimatedPayments"/> + <fg-show path="/vaPriorYearOverpaymentApplied"/> + <fg-show path="/vaExtensionPayments"/></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Line 27, tax you owe before use tax</th><td><fg-show path="/vaTaxYouOwe"/></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Line 28, your overpayment</th><td><fg-show path="/vaOverpayment"/></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Line 33, consumer's use tax</th><td><fg-show path="/vaUseTax"/></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Line 35, amount you owe</th><td><strong><fg-show path="/vaAmountYouOwe"/></strong></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Line 36, your refund</th><td><strong><fg-show path="/vaRefund"/></strong></td></tr>
        </table>
        <div class="df-screen" condition="/vaIsBelowFilingThreshold" operator="isTrue">
          <p>Your Virginia adjusted gross income is below the filing threshold for your filing status (§ 58.1-321), so your Virginia income tax is $0.</p>
        </div>
        <p><strong>Before anyone files this.</strong> Form 760 is signed under penalty of law as true, correct and complete. Virginia Tax compares the W-2 wages and withholding on a return with what the employer reported. The four answers above cannot be checked against any report, which is why they are asked and signed for.</p>
        <p>Lines not shown are out of scope for this prototype and taken as $0. Nothing here is filed.</p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </page>
</FlowConfig>
